{
  "abstract": "We estimate the cost of subtracting a mixed-pole model from a quadratic-twisted cubic two-mark correlation over the Eisenstein integers. The columns are squarefree, the Fourier numerator is natural, and the row weight is the actual convolution of a squarefree window with an unrestricted window. For column-product length $Q$, row length $X\\asymp Q^2$, and quadratic conductor norm $K$, the complete signed coprimality projector satisfies a subtraction bound $\\ll_\\epsilon (KXQND)^\\epsilon(K^{1/2}Q^{3/2}+Q)$, including both cross terms. With the motivating outside factor $D_0/Q$, the cost is $D_0(1+\\sqrt{KQ})$ up to subsidiary powers.\n\nThe proof applies established exceptional-Levi Whittaker theory to the classical quadratic minor source and retains the corrected Kazhdan–Patterson normalization. Both linked central quadratic L-values occur. Exact numerator deletion leaves sparse support of the form $rv^3$ and first-power decay, which make a divisor-weighted mean affordable by the quadratic fourth moment. An exact finite collision inverse preserves the numerator shift, Gauss phases, both mark lengths and all character zeros. A finite-ring shell calculation includes every dyadic numerator valuation in the specified quadratic packet. The ordinary natural-row cubic sieve then pays the cross terms.\n\nReproducible CPU checks validate local identities and the finite inverse, not the analytic theorem. The subtraction is defined through a finite residue construction; no uniform spectral expansion or useful signed bound for its complement is asserted. The remaining correlation is unresolved, so the result does not improve the total paid cutoff or establish a new zero-free region or RH.",
